MISA v0.1 — Empirical Specification

Minimal Interventionally Sufficient Abstraction

A Formal and Falsifiable Framework for Domain-Based Abstraction Validity

Research Concept — Version 0.1

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

Research Collaboration: Human + AI Collective : chatGpt and Deepseek 



Abstract

This specification defines an empirical program for testing the Minimal Interventionally Sufficient Abstraction (MISA) framework. The central hypothesis is that abstraction validity should not be represented by a single scalar property. Rather, validity is a domain indexed by the outcome of interest, intervention set, temporal horizon, tolerance, reference model, and outcome metric.

Let  S_{\mathrm{ref}}  denote an operational reference model,  O  an outcome of interest,  I  a set of admissible interventions,  \mathcal A  an abstraction, and  d_O  an outcome-specific discrepancy metric. MISA defines interventionally relevant validity through the approximation

\phi\!\left( \operatorname{do}(i)(S_{\mathrm{ref}}) \right) \;\approx\; \operatorname{do}{\mathcal A}(i)\!\left( \phi(S{\mathrm{ref}}) \right),

with validity determined by an outcome-specific tolerance. The empirical object is therefore not merely  \mathrm{Valid}(\mathcal A) , but

\boxed{ \mathrm{Valid} \left( \mathcal A \mid S_{\mathrm{ref}},O,I,T,\epsilon,d_O \right) }

and the corresponding validity domain

\mathfrak V(\mathcal A) = \left\{ (S_{\mathrm{ref}},O,I,T,\epsilon,d_O): \mathcal A \text{ satisfies the validity criterion} \right\}.

The first benchmark uses a two-dimensional coupled linear dynamical system with controllable cross-coupling parameter  \kappa . Three abstractions are compared: a one-variable abstraction, a joint state abstraction, and a reduced slow-mode abstraction. The benchmark tests whether increasing causal coupling systematically contracts the validity domain of independently modeled variables, whether reduced sufficient statistics exhibit a measurable intermediate regime, whether closed-loop validity contracts more rapidly with horizon than single-step validity, and whether the proposed causal-separability condition predicts the empirical boundary of independent abstraction.

The framework is explicitly falsifiable. No abstraction is assumed to be universally correct, and MISA is not claimed to identify a unique optimal representation.

---

1. Core Definitions

1.1 Reference Model

Let  S_{\mathrm{ref}}  be the operational reference model against which an abstraction is evaluated. The reference model is not assumed to be ontologically complete. It is a model selected for a specified experimental purpose.

This distinction is fundamental:

S_{\mathrm{true}} \rightarrow S_{\mathrm{ref}} \rightarrow \mathcal A.

The experiment therefore evaluates  \operatorname{Valid}(\mathcal A\mid S_{\mathrm{ref}})  rather than claiming direct access to truth. Two reference models may be used:  S_{\mathrm{ref}}^{(1)}  for an exact simulator or analytically known system, and  S_{\mathrm{ref}}^{(2)}  for a deliberately imperfect but operationally realistic model. The discrepancy between them estimates the sensitivity of the validity profile to reference-model error. This operationalizes the reference-model regress identified in causal abstraction literature, where any transformation between causal models presupposes an interpretation of interventions that may itself require a lower-level model.

1.2 Outcome

Let  O  denote the quantity whose preservation matters. The outcome may be a state variable, a trajectory, a prediction, a safety property, a regulation error, a control objective, or a vector of such quantities. An outcome-specific discrepancy metric is defined as  d_O(y,\hat y) . Different choices of  d_O  may generate different validity domains. Therefore:

\boxed{ \text{Abstraction validity is outcome-relative.} }

1.3 Intervention Set

Let  I  denote the set of interventions under consideration. An intervention may modify  i=(i_1,\ldots,i_m) . Examples include parameter changes, external inputs, state perturbations, actuator commands, model interventions, or policy actions. The intervention set is not necessarily externally fixed. For a specified outcome  O , define the required intervention set  I_{\mathrm{req}}(O) . An abstraction is useful only if its valid intervention domain contains the interventions required by the task:

\boxed{ I_{\mathrm{req}}(O) \subseteq I_\epsilon(\mathcal A) }

where  I_\epsilon(\mathcal A)  is the empirically determined validity domain.

1.4 Abstraction

An abstraction is represented by  \mathcal A=(X_A,F_A,\phi) , where  X_A  is the abstract state,  F_A  is the abstract dynamics, and  \phi  is the mapping from reference states to abstract states. The reference system evolves according to  S_{t+1}=F(S_t,i_t) . The abstraction evolves according to  A_{t+1}=F_A(A_t,i_t^A) . The central requirement is approximate intervention commutation:

\boxed{ \phi \left( \operatorname{do}(i)(S_{\mathrm{ref}}) \right) \approx \operatorname{do}{A}(i) \left( \phi(S{\mathrm{ref}}) \right). }

This is the standard commuting-diagram condition in causal abstraction theory, where a high-level causal model is an abstraction of a low-level model if interventions commute with the abstraction map.

1.5 Intervention Error

Define the intervention discrepancy

\Delta_{\mathcal A}(i,T) = d_O \left[ \phi \left( \operatorname{do}(i,S_{\mathrm{ref}},T) \right), \operatorname{do}{A} \left( i,\phi(S{\mathrm{ref}}),T \right) \right].

The abstraction is valid for intervention  i  when  \Delta_{\mathcal A}(i,T)\leq\epsilon . Hence

\boxed{ I_\epsilon(\mathcal A,T) = \left\{ i: \Delta_{\mathcal A}(i,T)\leq\epsilon \right\}. }

This set, rather than a scalar accuracy score, is the primary empirical object.

1.6 Validity Domain

Define the complete validity profile:

\boxed{ \mathfrak V(\mathcal A) = \left\{ (S_{\mathrm{ref}},O,I,T,\epsilon,d_O): \Delta_{\mathcal A}\leq\epsilon \right\}. }

Two abstractions therefore need not be ordered globally. One may satisfy the task over one intervention domain while another is valid over a different domain. Consequently,  \mathcal A_1\prec\mathcal A_2  is generally undefined without specifying outcome, intervention domain, horizon, tolerance, reference model, and complexity criterion.

---

2. MISA Selection

Let  C(\mathcal A)  denote abstraction complexity. Complexity may incorporate:

C = C_{\mathrm{state}} + C_{\mathrm{model}} + C_{\mathrm{compute}} + C_{\mathrm{intervention}} + C_{\mathrm{validation}}.

The last term is important experimentally. A model that is cheap to execute but extremely expensive to validate may not be minimal in an engineering sense. MISA is therefore defined as the feasible Pareto family

\boxed{ \mathcal A^* \in \operatorname{Pareto} \left[ C(\mathcal A), \operatorname{Coverage}(\mathfrak V(\mathcal A)) \right] }

subject to  I_{\mathrm{req}}(O) \subseteq I_\epsilon(\mathcal A) . Thus:

\boxed{ \text{MISA is a Pareto family, not a unique abstraction.} }

---

3. Safety Validity

Mean error is insufficient for safety-critical applications. Define a worst-case criterion:

\sup_{i\in I_{\mathrm{safe}}} \Delta_{\mathcal A}(i,T) \leq \epsilon_{\max}.

Alternatively, a tail-risk criterion may be used:

\operatorname{CVaR}{q} \left[ \Delta{\mathcal A} \right] \leq \epsilon_q.

The distinction is important:  \mathcal A^_{\mathrm{average}} \neq \mathcal A^_{\mathrm{safe}}  in general. Risk tolerance therefore defines which region of the Pareto frontier is admissible.

---

4. Statistical Estimation

In empirical applications,  \Delta_{\mathcal A}(i,T)  is unknown. It must be estimated from finite samples. For intervention  i , collect  n_i  independent or appropriately blocked trials. Estimate  \widehat{\Delta}_{\mathcal A}(i,T) . Construct a confidence interval  [L_i,U_i] . A conservative empirical validity rule is  U_i\leq\epsilon . Therefore define

\boxed{ \widehat I_{\epsilon,\alpha} = \left\{ i: U_i\leq\epsilon \right\}. }

The upper confidence bound is deliberately used because declaring validity requires evidence that the error is below tolerance.

4.1 Multiple Comparisons

Containment is not a single statistical test. For  I_{\mathrm{req}} = \{i_1,\ldots,i_m\} , there are  m  simultaneous validity conditions. A naïve collection of  1-\alpha  confidence intervals does not guarantee joint coverage. Therefore the experiment must construct simultaneous confidence bounds satisfying

\Pr \left[ \forall i\in I_{\mathrm{req}}, \Delta_{\mathcal A}(i,T) \leq U_i \right] \geq 1-\beta.

The empirical containment criterion becomes

\boxed{ \Pr \left[ I_{\mathrm{req}} \subseteq \widehat I_{\epsilon,\alpha} \right] \geq 1-\beta. }

Bonferroni, Holm, simultaneous bootstrap procedures, or other appropriate family-wise methods may be compared. FDR procedures may be useful for exploratory validity mapping, but safety-oriented containment should prioritize simultaneous error control.

---

5. Closed-Loop Validity

Single interventions are insufficient for control systems. Let  \pi  be a policy generating interventions:  i_t=\pi(a_t,o_t) . Define policy-level discrepancy  \Delta_{\mathcal A}(\pi,T) . The policy-validity domain is

\boxed{ \Pi_\epsilon(\mathcal A,T) = \left\{ \pi: \Delta_{\mathcal A}(\pi,T) \leq\epsilon \right\}. }

A control abstraction is adequate when

\boxed{ \Pi_{\mathrm{req}}(O,T) \subseteq \Pi_\epsilon(\mathcal A,T). }

This condition is stronger than single-step validity. It permits the empirical hypothesis:

\operatorname{Coverage} \left[ \Pi_\epsilon(\mathcal A,T_2) \right] \leq \operatorname{Coverage} \left[ \Pi_\epsilon(\mathcal A,T_1) \right]

for  T_2>T_1 , for abstractions whose approximation errors accumulate over time.

---

6. Composition

Consider two independently abstracted subsystems  X_1  and  X_2 . Their independent abstractions are compositionally valid only if cross-interventions remain sufficiently small at the outcome level. A local diagnostic is

\left\| \frac{\partial X_2}{\partial i_1} \right\|_O \leq \epsilon

and

\left\| \frac{\partial X_1}{\partial i_2} \right\|_O \leq \epsilon.

More generally, define a cross-intervention matrix

J_{\mathrm{cross}} = \begin{bmatrix} \partial X_1/\partial i_2 \\ \partial X_2/\partial i_1 \end{bmatrix}.

The hypothesis is that the norm of this matrix predicts the contraction of independent-abstraction validity. If cross-effects exceed the outcome tolerance, a joint abstraction may become necessary. This connects to causal abstraction literature where local causal validity does not automatically imply global compositional validity.

---

7. Quantitative Coupling Hypothesis

The experiment tests whether abstraction complexity is systematically related to hidden causal coupling. Let

H(\mathcal A) = \left\| \frac{\partial X_{\mathrm{hidden}}}{\partial i} \right\|_O.

The empirical relationship is modeled as  C(\mathcal A^*) \gtrsim g(H) , where  g'(H)\geq0 . The experiment does not assume that  g  is linear. Candidate regimes include:

· Linear:  C\approx c_0+c_1H 
· Superlinear:  C\approx c_0+c_1H+c_2H^2 
· Threshold:  C(H) \approx \begin{cases} C_0,&H<H_c \\ C_1,&H\geq H_c. \end{cases} 

The functional relationship is therefore itself an empirical question. This converts the qualitative claim that stronger coupling requires richer abstraction into a falsifiable quantitative hypothesis.

---

8. Synthetic Benchmark

8.1 Reference Dynamics

Consider

\begin{aligned} x_{1,t+1} &= a_1x_{1,t} +b_1i_{1,t} +\kappa x_{2,t}, \\ x_{2,t+1} &= a_2x_{2,t} +b_2i_{2,t} +\kappa x_{1,t}. \end{aligned}

The coupling parameter  \kappa  is systematically swept. For sufficiently small  \kappa , the subsystems approach independent behavior. For larger  \kappa , the independent approximation progressively loses validity. The exact simulator constitutes  S_{\mathrm{ref}}^{(1)} .

8.2 Candidate Abstractions

Abstraction A1 — Independent Variable

\mathcal A_1: \quad x_1

with  x_2  treated as an unmodeled disturbance. The model is

\hat x_{1,t+1} = a_1x_{1,t}+b_1i_{1,t}.

Abstraction A2 — Joint State

\mathcal A_2: \quad (x_1,x_2).

The complete coupled dynamics are retained. This abstraction provides the reference upper-complexity baseline.

Abstraction A3 — Reduced Slow Mode

Diagonalize the coupled system:  x_{t+1}=Mx_t+Bi_t . Let  M=V\Lambda V^{-1} . The transformed coordinates  z=V^{-1}x  represent system modes. If one eigenmode dominates the relevant temporal behavior, define  \mathcal A_3: \quad z_{\mathrm{slow}} . This abstraction tests whether a lower-dimensional sufficient representation can preserve the relevant outcome despite strong coupling.

8.3 Outcomes

Three outcomes are proposed:

· Outcome O1 — One-Step Prediction:  O_1=x_{1,T}, \qquad T=1. 
· Outcome O2 — Long-Horizon State:  O_2=(x_{1,T},x_{2,T}), \qquad T=10. 
· Outcome O3 — Closed-Loop Regulation: A policy  \pi  controls the system toward  x_1^*=0 . The outcome metric may include

J = \sum_{t=0}^{T} \left[ q x_{1,t}^2 + r i_{1,t}^2 \right].

8.4 Experimental Matrix

For each  (\mathcal A_k,O_j,\kappa,T) , estimate  \widehat{\Delta}_{\mathcal A_k} . Then calculate  \widehat I_{\epsilon,\alpha} (\mathcal A_k,O_j,\kappa,T)  and, for control,  \widehat\Pi_{\epsilon,\alpha} (\mathcal A_k,O_j,\kappa,T) . The resulting object is an empirical approximation of  \mathfrak V(\mathcal A_k) .

---

9. Primary Predictions

P1 — Coupling contraction. For the independent abstraction,

\frac{\partial}{\partial\kappa} \operatorname{Coverage} [ I_\epsilon(\mathcal A_1) ] <0

over the coupling regime where the approximation begins to fail.

P2 — Joint-model robustness. For  \mathcal A_2 ,  \operatorname{Coverage} [ I_\epsilon(\mathcal A_2) ]  should exhibit substantially lower sensitivity to  \kappa , subject to numerical conditioning and model-estimation error.

P3 — Intermediate reduced representation. For  \mathcal A_3 , an intermediate regime may exist in which  C(\mathcal A_3)<C(\mathcal A_2)  while  \operatorname{Coverage} [ \mathfrak V(\mathcal A_3) ]  remains comparable to the joint abstraction for the specified outcome. This prediction is falsified if the reduced representation provides no measurable validity–complexity advantage across the tested parameter regime.

P4 — Temporal contraction. For abstractions with accumulating approximation error,  \operatorname{Coverage} [ \Pi_\epsilon(\mathcal A,T) ]  should decline with increasing  T .

P5 — Composition boundary. The independently valid regime should approximately coincide with a region in which

\left\| \frac{\partial X_{\mathrm{hidden}}}{\partial i} \right\|_O \lesssim \epsilon.

Systematic disagreement between the predicted and observed boundary would challenge the proposed causal-separability criterion.

---

10. Reference-Model Experiment

Construct a second reference:

S_{\mathrm{ref}}^{(2)} = S_{\mathrm{ref}}^{(1)} + E_{\mathrm{model}},

where  E_{\mathrm{model}}  introduces controlled structural or parametric bias. For example,  \tilde\kappa=\kappa+\delta_\kappa  or a deliberately omitted weak interaction term. Calculate  \mathfrak V^{(1)}(\mathcal A)  and  \mathfrak V^{(2)}(\mathcal A) . Define validity-profile displacement

D_{\mathfrak V} = d_{\mathfrak V} \left( \mathfrak V^{(1)}, \mathfrak V^{(2)} \right).

The experiment therefore measures not only whether an abstraction is valid, but how sensitive its claimed validity is to the reference model. This operationalizes the reference-model regress rather than treating it philosophically.

---

11. Falsification Conditions

The framework should be considered empirically weakened if one or more of the following occur systematically.

F1 — No coupling effect. If  \mathfrak V(\mathcal A_1)  remains effectively unchanged as  \kappa  increases despite increasing cross-causal influence, the proposed coupling-validity relationship is unsupported.

F2 — No reduced-model regime. If  \mathcal A_3  never achieves a meaningful validity–complexity tradeoff relative to  \mathcal A_1,\mathcal A_2 , the reduced-sufficient-statistic component of MISA is unsupported in this benchmark.

F3 — Composition criterion failure. If independent abstraction remains valid far beyond the predicted cross-intervention boundary, the separability criterion is overly conservative. If validity fails substantially before the predicted boundary, the criterion is insufficient.

F4 — Temporal prediction failure. If closed-loop validity does not exhibit the predicted relationship with horizon under conditions where approximation errors theoretically accumulate, the temporal extension requires revision.

F5 — Domain representation provides no advantage. If replacing scalar accuracy with validity-domain representation produces no additional explanatory or engineering information relative to conventional scalar model evaluation, the central methodological claim is weakened.

---

12. The Central Empirical Question

The experiment is therefore not asking: Which abstraction is best? It asks:

\boxed{ \text{For which outcomes, interventions, horizons, tolerances, and references is each abstraction valid?} }

and then:

\boxed{ \text{What is the minimum complexity required to cover the intervention domain actually required by the task?} }

This is the operational meaning of MISA.

---

13. Methodological Claim

The paper does not claim that MISA is the universal theory of abstraction. Instead, it advances the narrower hypothesis:

\boxed{ \text{Abstraction validity is better represented as a domain than as a scalar, and abstraction selection is therefore a constrained Pareto problem over intervention coverage and complexity.} }

The claim is falsifiable because the domain representation may fail to provide predictive, explanatory, or engineering value over conventional scalar evaluation.

---

14. General Design Principle

The empirical framework is summarized by six principles:

\boxed{ \begin{aligned} &\text{Reduction constrains possibility.} \\ &\text{Intervention defines relevance.} \\ &\text{Outcome defines adequacy.} \\ &\text{Substrate constrains realizability.} \\ &\text{Risk defines acceptable failure.} \\ &\text{Validation maps the domain of validity.} \end{aligned} }

And the corresponding selection principle is:

\boxed{ \text{MISA is a Pareto family characterized by a validity domain, not a universal optimal abstraction.} }

---

15. Research Sequence

The empirical program should proceed in the following order:

\boxed{ \text{Analytic benchmark} \rightarrow \text{Monte Carlo estimation} \rightarrow \text{statistical validation} \rightarrow \text{closed-loop test} \rightarrow \text{imperfect reference} \rightarrow \text{real system} }

Only after the synthetic system survives these tests should the framework be instantiated in:

· LLM mechanistic/causal abstraction (using established benchmarks such as MIB for circuit and causal variable localization)
· Robotic control
· Acoustic levitation

The synthetic benchmark provides the cleanest environment in which a failure can be attributed to the framework rather than to measurement noise, undocumented system complexity, or uncertainty about the physical reference.

---

Conclusion

MISA becomes scientifically meaningful only when its validity claims can fail. The benchmark therefore treats abstraction as a measurable object with state, interventions, outcomes, time, risk, and reference. The resulting object is not a universal score. It is a map:

\boxed{ \mathfrak V(\mathcal A) : (O,I,T,\epsilon,S_{\mathrm{ref}},d_O) \mapsto \{\mathrm{valid},\mathrm{invalid}\}. }

The central empirical hypothesis is consequently:

\boxed{ \text{A useful abstraction is not the most accurate representation of the system.} }

Rather,

\boxed{ \text{A useful abstraction is the least complex representation whose validated intervention domain contains the interventions required by the outcome.} }

That proposition can now be measured, compared, and falsified.

Appendices to MISA v0.1 — Empirical Specification


Appendix A: Mathematical Preliminaries

A.1 Structural Causal Models (SCMs)

A structural causal model (SCM) is a tuple

\mathcal M = \langle \mathcal U, \mathcal V, \mathcal F, P(\mathcal U) \rangle

where \mathcal U is a set of exogenous (background) variables, \mathcal V is a set of endogenous (observed) variables, \mathcal F is a set of structural functions f_V: \mathcal U_V \times \mathcal V_{\mathrm{pa}(V)} \to \mathcal V that assign values to each endogenous variable given its causal parents and exogenous inputs, and P(\mathcal U) is a probability distribution over exogenous variables. Each SCM induces a directed acyclic graph (DAG) where edges represent direct causal influence.

A.2 The Do-Operator and Intervention Semantics

The do-operator, introduced by Pearl (1995, 2000), represents a physical intervention that sets a variable X to a specific value x regardless of its normal causes. Formally, \operatorname{do}(X=x) modifies the SCM by replacing the structural equation for X with the constant X=x, removing all incoming edges to X in the associated DAG. This corresponds to the second layer of Pearl's causal hierarchy (intervention), distinct from observational conditioning P(Y \mid X=x).

A.3 Pearl's Causal Hierarchy (PCH)

Any fully specified SCM induces a collection of distributions known as the Pearl Causal Hierarchy, which delimits three cognitive layers:

Layer Name Query Type Mathematical Object
1 Association P(Y \mid X) Conditional distribution
2 Intervention P(Y \mid \operatorname{do}(X)) Interventional distribution
3 Counterfactual P(Y_x \mid X', Y') Counterfactual distribution

MISA operates primarily at Layer 2 (intervention) for single-intervention validity, and requires Layer 3 (counterfactual) for closed-loop policy validity. The commuting-diagram condition is a statement about Layer 2: whether \phi and \operatorname{do}(\cdot) commute.

A.4 Markov Categories and Causal Abstraction

Recent work has placed causal abstraction within the framework of Markov categories, where a causal abstraction is defined as a natural transformation between appropriate Markov functors. This categorical perspective unifies previous definitions by Rubenstein et al. (2017) and Beckers and Halpern (2019), and handles deterministic, probabilistic, discrete, continuous, and mixed-variable cases. For MISA, the categorical formulation provides a rigorous foundation for the commuting-diagram condition and for composition properties.

---

Appendix B: Causal Abstraction Theory

B.1 The Commuting Diagram Condition

Rubenstein et al. (2017) introduced the notion of an exact transformation between SCMs as a pair of maps (\tau, \omega) where \tau maps low-level variables to high-level variables and \omega maps low-level interventions to high-level interventions, such that the following diagram commutes for every intervention i \in \mathcal I_L:

\begin{array}{ccc}
S_{\mathrm{ref}} & \xrightarrow{\operatorname{do}(i)} & S_{\mathrm{ref}}' \\[6pt]
\big\downarrow{\scriptstyle\phi} & & \big\downarrow{\scriptstyle\phi} \\[6pt]
A & \xrightarrow{\operatorname{do}(\omega(i))} & A'
\end{array}

Formally, this requires

\phi\bigl(\operatorname{do}(i)(S_{\mathrm{ref}})\bigr) = \operatorname{do}(\omega(i))\bigl(\phi(S_{\mathrm{ref}})\bigr)

for all i in the domain of \omega. MISA weakens this to approximate commutation with tolerance \epsilon and restricts attention to an outcome-relevant distance d_O:

d_O\Bigl[\phi\bigl(\operatorname{do}(i)(S_{\mathrm{ref}})\bigr),\; \operatorname{do}(\omega(i))\bigl(\phi(S_{\mathrm{ref}})\bigr)\Bigr] \leq \epsilon.

B.2 Constructive vs. Strong Abstraction

Beckers and Halpern (2019) distinguish between strong \tau-abstractions and constructive \tau-abstractions. A constructive abstraction requires an explicit alignment between high-level variables and subsets of low-level variables, ensuring that the abstraction is not merely a distributional coincidence but reflects a genuine structural correspondence. MISA adopts the constructive stance implicitly: the map \phi must be interpretable, and the intervention correspondence \omega must be explicit. This rules out degenerate abstractions that happen to commute for individual interventions but fail under joint or sequential interventions.

B.3 The Reference-Model Regress

A foundational issue in causal abstraction theory is the infinite regress of intervention interpretation. To determine whether a low-level action d_L constitutes a valid intervention, one presumably needs a model at an even lower level, leading to an infinite regress. In practice, the regress is halted at a level where the intervention semantics are unambiguous—for example, at the level of neural network activations in mechanistic interpretability, where "intervention" means patching or ablating a specific activation. MISA operationalizes this by explicitly indexing validity claims by the reference model S_{\mathrm{ref}}, acknowledging that no validity claim is absolute.

B.4 Compositional Validity

Causal abstractions are compositional in the categorical sense: if F' is an abstraction of F and F'' is an abstraction of F', then F'' is an abstraction of F. However, composition of independently valid abstractions is not guaranteed. Local causal pieces that agree wherever they overlap can still fail to assemble into a global model. MISA therefore treats composition validity as a separate condition requiring cross-intervention analysis.

---

Appendix C: Statistical Estimation Theory

C.1 Confidence Intervals for Intervention Discrepancy

For each intervention i, the true discrepancy \Delta_{\mathcal A}(i,T) is unknown and must be estimated from n_i independent trials. Let \widehat{\Delta}_{\mathcal A}(i,T) be the sample estimate. A (1-\alpha)-level confidence interval [L_i, U_i] satisfies

\Pr\bigl(L_i \leq \Delta_{\mathcal A}(i,T) \leq U_i\bigr) \geq 1-\alpha.

The conservative empirical validity rule U_i \leq \epsilon ensures that declaring validity requires evidence that the error is below tolerance, with confidence 1-\alpha. Common interval constructions include Wald intervals, percentile bootstrap intervals, and bias-corrected accelerated (BCa) intervals.

C.2 Simultaneous Confidence Bands

The containment test I_{\mathrm{req}} \subseteq \widehat I_{\epsilon,\alpha} involves m = |I_{\mathrm{req}}| simultaneous conditions. A collection of pointwise 1-\alpha intervals does not guarantee joint coverage. The family-wise coverage requirement is

\Pr\left[\forall i \in I_{\mathrm{req}}: \Delta_{\mathcal A}(i,T) \leq U_i\right] \geq 1-\beta.

Several procedures are available:

1. Bonferroni correction: use \alpha' = \alpha/m for each interval. Conservative but simple.
2. Holm step-down: a sequentially rejective procedure that maintains family-wise error rate while improving power over Bonferroni.
3. Simultaneous bootstrap (sup-t): constructs rectangular confidence bands by modifying the confidence level so that the intersection of all adjusted intervals contains the parameter vector with specified coverage. This is always less conservative than Bonferroni or Holm.
4. Romano–Wolf stepdown: a resampling-based procedure that constructs rectangular simultaneous confidence intervals in subsequent steps while keeping coverage above 1-\alpha.

For safety-oriented containment, simultaneous error control is mandatory. FDR procedures may be used for exploratory validity mapping but are insufficient for safety claims.

C.3 Bootstrap Implementation

The fractional weighted bootstrap (fwb) package provides an implementation of simultaneous confidence intervals using the sup-t band, with support for Wald, percentile, BCa, and normal approximation intervals. The simultaneous adjustment is computed by inverting the simultaneous bands, and is available for Wald and percentile interval types. For the MISA benchmark, we recommend the percentile simultaneous bootstrap with B = 10{,}000 replications, as it does not require distributional assumptions and handles the nonlinearity of \Delta_{\mathcal A} naturally.

---

Appendix D: Safety and Risk Measures

D.1 Worst-Case Criterion

The worst-case validity condition is

\sup_{i \in I_{\mathrm{safe}}} \Delta_{\mathcal A}(i,T) \leq \epsilon_{\max}.

This is the most conservative formulation: every intervention in the safety-critical set must satisfy the tolerance. It is appropriate when the intervention set is finite and enumerable, and when any single failure is unacceptable.

D.2 Conditional Value-at-Risk (CVaR)

When worst-case conservatism is excessive, a tail-risk criterion is preferable. Let \Delta_{\mathcal A} be a random variable over interventions i \sim P_I. The Value-at-Risk at level q \in (0,1) is

\operatorname{VaR}_q[\Delta_{\mathcal A}] = \inf\{t : \Pr[\Delta_{\mathcal A} \leq t] \geq q\}.

The Conditional Value-at-Risk (CVaR) at level q is the expected value of the worst (1-q) fraction of outcomes:

\operatorname{CVaR}_q[\Delta_{\mathcal A}] = \mathbb E\bigl[\Delta_{\mathcal A} \mid \Delta_{\mathcal A} \geq \operatorname{VaR}_q[\Delta_{\mathcal A}]\bigr].

CVaR is a coherent risk measure—it satisfies monotonicity, sub-additivity, positive homogeneity, and translation invariance—which makes it well-justified from a decision-theoretic perspective for risk-sensitive safe sets.

For safety-critical applications, the CVaR condition is

\operatorname{CVaR}_q[\Delta_{\mathcal A}] \leq \epsilon_q.

The relationship between average-valid and safety-valid abstractions is generically

\mathcal A^*_{\mathrm{average}} \neq \mathcal A^*_{\mathrm{safe}},

and the Pareto frontier between complexity and safety margin is the object of interest.

D.3 Distributionally Robust CVaR

When the intervention distribution P_I is itself uncertain, the worst-case CVaR over a moment-based ambiguity set (fixed mean and covariance) provides a distributionally robust safety condition. This is directly relevant to MISA when deployment interventions may differ from validation interventions.

---

Appendix E: Composition and Separability

E.1 Cross-Intervention Jacobian

For two subsystems X_1 and X_2, define the cross-intervention Jacobian

J_{\mathrm{cross}} = \begin{bmatrix} \dfrac{\partial X_1}{\partial i_2} & \dfrac{\partial X_2}{\partial i_1} \end{bmatrix}.

The composition validity condition is that the outcome-relevant norm of J_{\mathrm{cross}} is below the tolerance:

\|J_{\mathrm{cross}}\|_O \leq \epsilon.

This is a local linearization; for nonlinear systems, the condition should be checked over the entire intervention domain I_1 \times I_2, not merely at a single operating point.

E.2 Composition Theorem (Conjecture)

Conjecture E.1. Let \mathcal A_1 be valid for I_1 with tolerance \epsilon_1, and \mathcal A_2 be valid for I_2 with tolerance \epsilon_2. Then \mathcal A_1 \oplus \mathcal A_2 is valid for I_1 \times I_2 with tolerance \epsilon_1 + \epsilon_2 + \|J_{\mathrm{cross}}\|_O, provided that \|J_{\mathrm{cross}}\|_O \leq \epsilon_{\mathrm{cross}}.

This conjecture is deliberately conservative: it requires the cross-coupling to be small relative to the tolerances. A sharper version would replace the sum with a more refined bound depending on the structure of the abstraction maps.

E.3 Failure Mode: Cross-Intervention Leakage

The composition condition fails when an intervention in I_1 changes the state variables that \mathcal A_2 depends on, or vice versa. This is cross-intervention leakage. The diagnostic is

\frac{\partial X_2}{\partial i_1} \neq 0

at the outcome level. If this derivative is non-negligible relative to \epsilon, independent abstraction is invalid and a joint abstraction is required.

---

Appendix F: Quantitative Coupling Hypothesis

F.1 Hidden Coupling Measure

Let X_{\mathrm{hidden}} denote the state not exposed by abstraction \mathcal A. The hidden coupling is

H(\mathcal A) = \left\| \frac{\partial X_{\mathrm{hidden}}}{\partial i} \right\|_O.

This measures how strongly interventions affect the unmodeled degrees of freedom, weighted by their outcome relevance.

F.2 Candidate Functional Forms

The relationship between complexity and hidden coupling is modeled as C(\mathcal A^*) \gtrsim g(H), where g is monotone non-decreasing. Candidate regimes:

Regime Functional Form Interpretation
Linear C \approx c_0 + c_1 H Each unit of coupling costs a fixed amount of complexity
Superlinear C \approx c_0 + c_1 H + c_2 H^2 Complexity grows faster than coupling; there are nonlinear interactions
Threshold C(H) = C_0 for H < H_c, C_1 for H \geq H_c A phase transition: below H_c, a simple abstraction suffices; above, a rich abstraction is required

The functional form is itself an empirical question to be tested in the benchmark.

