Appendix G: Reference-Model Perturbation Analysis

G.1 Two-Reference Design

The reference model S_{\mathrm{ref}}^{(1)} is the exact simulator. The perturbed reference is

S_{\mathrm{ref}}^{(2)} = S_{\mathrm{ref}}^{(1)} + E_{\mathrm{model}},

where E_{\mathrm{model}} is a controlled structural or parametric bias. For the linear benchmark, this can be implemented as

\tilde\kappa = \kappa + \delta_\kappa

or as an omitted weak interaction term +\eta x_1^2 or +\eta x_2^2.

G.2 Validity-Profile Displacement

Let \mathfrak V^{(1)}(\mathcal A) and \mathfrak V^{(2)}(\mathcal A) be the validity domains computed against S_{\mathrm{ref}}^{(1)} and S_{\mathrm{ref}}^{(2)} respectively. Define the displacement

D_{\mathfrak V} = d_{\mathfrak V}\bigl(\mathfrak V^{(1)}, \mathfrak V^{(2)}\bigr),

where d_{\mathfrak V} is a suitable metric on the space of validity domains (e.g., the symmetric difference measure of the intervention sets, or the Hausdorff distance between the validity boundaries). This quantifies how sensitive the claimed validity is to the choice of reference model.

G.3 Interpretation

A small D_{\mathfrak V} indicates that the abstraction's validity profile is robust to reference-model error. A large D_{\mathfrak V} indicates that the validity claim is fragile and should be treated with caution. This operationalizes the reference-model regress as a measurable quantity rather than a philosophical caveat.

