Appendix H: Analytical Solutions for the Linear Benchmark

H.1 System Matrices

The coupled linear system is

\begin{aligned}
x_{1,t+1} &= a_1 x_{1,t} + b_1 i_{1,t} + \kappa x_{2,t}, \\
x_{2,t+1} &= a_2 x_{2,t} + b_2 i_{2,t} + \kappa x_{1,t}.
\end{aligned}

In matrix form:

\begin{bmatrix} x_{1,t+1} \\ x_{2,t+1} \end{bmatrix}
=
\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix} a_1 & \kappa \\ \kappa & a_2 \end{bmatrix}}_{M}
\begin{bmatrix} x_{1,t} \\ x_{2,t} \end{bmatrix}
+
\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix} b_1 & 0 \\ 0 & b_2 \end{bmatrix}}_{B}
\begin{bmatrix} i_{1,t} \\ i_{2,t} \end{bmatrix}.

H.2 Eigenvalue Decomposition

The eigenvalues of M are

\lambda_{\pm} = \frac{(a_1 + a_2) \pm \sqrt{(a_1 - a_2)^2 + 4\kappa^2}}{2}.

The corresponding eigenvectors form the columns of V:

M = V \Lambda V^{-1}, \qquad
\Lambda = \operatorname{diag}(\lambda_+, \lambda_-).

For \kappa = 0, \lambda_+ = \max(a_1, a_2) and \lambda_- = \min(a_1, a_2). As \kappa increases, the eigenvalues separate further (for a_1 \neq a_2) and the eigenvectors rotate.

H.3 Slow-Mode Approximation

The transformed coordinates z = V^{-1} x evolve independently:

z_{t+1} = \Lambda z_t + V^{-1} B i_t.

If |\lambda_-| \ll |\lambda_+|, the slow mode z_- dominates the long-horizon behavior. The reduced abstraction \mathcal A_3 retains only z_-, discarding z_+. The validity of this approximation depends on:

1. The separation ratio |\lambda_+|/|\lambda_-|,
2. The projection of the intervention B i onto the fast mode,
3. The outcome metric d_O.

For T \to \infty, the fast mode decays as |\lambda_+|^T, so the reduced abstraction becomes asymptotically exact provided the fast mode is stable (|\lambda_+| < 1) and the intervention does not persistently excite it.

H.4 Closed-Loop Analysis

For the regulation outcome O_3, consider a linear state-feedback policy

i_t = -K x_t

designed on the abstraction. The closed-loop dynamics under the abstraction are

A_{t+1} = (F_A - B_A K) A_t.

The true closed-loop dynamics are

x_{t+1} = (M - B K) x_t.

The policy-level discrepancy

\Delta_{\mathcal A}(\pi, T)
=
d_O\left[
\phi\bigl((M - BK)^T x_0\bigr),\;
(F_A - B_A K)^T \phi(x_0)
\right]

grows with T if the abstraction's closed-loop eigenvalues differ from the true closed-loop eigenvalues. Stability in \mathcal A does not imply stability in S_{\mathrm{ref}}: if the abstraction misses a fast unstable mode, the true system may diverge even when the abstract system is stable.

