Appendix K: Extended Falsification Conditions

K.1 F1 — No Coupling Effect

Statement: If \operatorname{Coverage}[I_\epsilon(\mathcal A_1)] does not decrease with \kappa for any outcome O_j or horizon T, the coupling-validity relationship is unsupported.

Statistical test: Regress coverage on \kappa and test whether the slope is significantly negative at the 0.05 level.

K.2 F2 — No Reduced-Model Regime

Statement: If \mathcal A_3 never achieves a coverage–complexity tradeoff superior to both \mathcal A_1 and \mathcal A_2, the reduced-sufficient-statistic component is unsupported.

Operationalization: Compute the Pareto frontier of (complexity, coverage) for all three abstractions. If \mathcal A_3 is never on the frontier, F2 is confirmed.

K.3 F3 — Composition Criterion Failure

Statement: If independent abstraction remains valid far beyond the predicted cross-intervention boundary \|J_{\mathrm{cross}}\|_O \leq \epsilon, the separability criterion is overly conservative. If validity fails substantially before the boundary, the criterion is insufficient.

Operationalization: Plot the empirical validity boundary of \mathcal A_1 \oplus \mathcal A_2 against \|J_{\mathrm{cross}}\|_O. Compute the distance between the predicted and observed boundaries. A distance greater than 2\epsilon in either direction confirms F3.

K.4 F4 — Temporal Prediction Failure

Statement: If \operatorname{Coverage}[\Pi_\epsilon(\mathcal A, T)] does not decrease with T for abstractions whose approximation errors theoretically accumulate, the temporal extension requires revision.

Operationalization: Fit a monotone decreasing function to coverage vs. T. If the fitted slope is not significantly negative for \mathcal A_1 and \mathcal A_2 in the coupling regime \kappa > 0.1, F4 is confirmed.

K.5 F5 — Domain Representation Provides No Advantage

Statement: If the validity-domain representation produces no additional explanatory or engineering information relative to scalar accuracy, the central methodological claim is weakened.

Operationalization: Compare the predictive power of the domain representation (which interventions are valid) against a scalar accuracy score for predicting out-of-sample intervention performance. Use cross-validation. If the domain representation does not improve prediction by at least 10\% in explained variance, F5 is confirmed.

