Appendix L: Relationship to Existing Frameworks

L.1 Good Regulator Theorem (Conant–Ashby)

The classical Good Regulator Theorem states that every good regulator of a system must be a model of that system. More precisely, any regulator that is maximally both successful and simple must be isomorphic with the system being regulated. However, the theorem has been criticized for only establishing that some good regulators are models, not all. Modern reformulations show that whenever an agent can perform a regulation task, an observer can interpret it as having "beliefs" about its environment.

MISA refines the Good Regulator intuition: the regulator does not need to model everything; it needs to model what the required intervention class demands, within tolerance. The MISA condition

I_{\mathrm{req}}(O) \subseteq I_\epsilon(\mathcal A)

is a sufficient condition for the regulator's model component to support the required interventions. This is not claimed as a theorem-level equivalence but as an engineering interpretation of the Good Regulator principle.

L.2 Effective Field Theory (EFT)

In quantum field theory, an effective field theory comes with a regime of validity and necessary cutoffs. The theory is valid only up to an energy scale suitably below the cutoff, and the cutoff implies a small parameter in which one can expand. Physics at shorter distances is captured by a more fundamental theory, but the EFT remains useful within its domain.

The parallel to MISA is direct: an abstraction is valid only within its validity domain I_\epsilon(\mathcal A), and the "cutoff" is the boundary of that domain. The EFT perspective that "there is no reason to demand that a field theory be a valid description to arbitrarily short distances" is the physics analogue of MISA's claim that no abstraction is universally valid.

L.3 Marr's Levels of Analysis

Marr (1982) distinguished three levels of analysis: the computational theory (what is the goal?), the representation and algorithm (how is it computed?), and the implementation (how is it physically realized?). Each level has its own adequacy conditions, constrained by the levels below. MISA generalizes this: the choice of abstraction is determined not by ontological depth but by the intervention class and outcome, with substrate compatibility as a hard constraint.

L.4 Pearl's Causal Hierarchy as a Validity Hierarchy

MISA's hierarchy of validity notions maps onto Pearl's causal hierarchy:

MISA Level Pearl Level Structure Required
State validity Association Observational distribution
Single-intervention validity Intervention Interventional distribution
Sequential validity Intervention + time Sequential interventional distribution
Closed-loop policy validity Counterfactual Counterfactual distribution

Each stronger claim requires preserving additional causal structure, and hence a stricter commuting condition.

