Appendix N: Proof Sketches

N.1 Composition Bound (Conjecture E.1) — Proof Sketch

Let \mathcal A_1 and \mathcal A_2 be abstractions of X_1 and X_2 respectively. For any (i_1, i_2) \in I_1 \times I_2,

\Delta_{\mathcal A_1 \oplus \mathcal A_2}(i_1, i_2)
=
d_O\Bigl[
\phi_{1\oplus 2}\bigl(\operatorname{do}(i_1, i_2)(S_{\mathrm{ref}})\bigr),\;
\operatorname{do}(i_1, i_2)\bigl(\phi_{1\oplus 2}(S_{\mathrm{ref}})\bigr)
\Bigr].

By the triangle inequality,

\Delta_{\mathcal A_1 \oplus \mathcal A_2}(i_1, i_2)
\leq
\Delta_{\mathcal A_1}(i_1) + \Delta_{\mathcal A_2}(i_2) + \|J_{\mathrm{cross}}\|_O.

The first two terms are bounded by \epsilon_1 and \epsilon_2 by the validity of \mathcal A_1 and \mathcal A_2. The third term is bounded by \epsilon_{\mathrm{cross}} by the separability condition. Hence the composition is valid with tolerance \epsilon_1 + \epsilon_2 + \epsilon_{\mathrm{cross}}. ∎

N.2 Slow-Mode Approximation — Error Bound

For the linear system with diagonalizable M = V \Lambda V^{-1}, the true state is

x_T = M^T x_0 + \sum_{t=0}^{T-1} M^{T-1-t} B i_t.

The slow-mode approximation retains only the \lambda_- component:

\hat x_T = V \operatorname{diag}(0, \lambda_-^T) V^{-1} x_0 + V \operatorname{diag}(0, \ldots) V^{-1} \sum_{t=0}^{T-1} \Lambda^{T-1-t} V^{-1} B i_t.

The approximation error is

\|x_T - \hat x_T\| \leq |\lambda_+|^T \|P_+ x_0\| + \sum_{t=0}^{T-1} |\lambda_+|^{T-1-t} \|P_+ B i_t\|,

where P_+ is the projector onto the fast eigenspace. This error decays as |\lambda_+|^T for stable fast modes, confirming that the reduced abstraction is asymptotically valid for long horizons.

N.3 Worst-Case vs. Average Validity Separation

Let \mathcal A_{\mathrm{avg}} minimize expected complexity subject to average validity, and \mathcal A_{\mathrm{safe}} minimize expected complexity subject to worst-case validity. In general, \mathcal A_{\mathrm{avg}} \neq \mathcal A_{\mathrm{safe}}. A counterexample: let \Delta_{\mathcal A}(i) be 0 for 99.9\% of interventions and 100\epsilon for 0.1\%. Then the average is 0.1\epsilon, so \mathcal A_{\mathrm{avg}} is valid on average. But the worst-case is 100\epsilon, so \mathcal A_{\mathrm{safe}} requires a richer abstraction. This establishes the strict separation.

