Appendix O: Extended Discussion of the Reference-Model Regress

The infinite regress of intervention interpretation is not a defect of MISA; it is a fundamental feature of causal abstraction theory. Every abstraction claim is indexed by a reference model, and every reference model is itself an abstraction. MISA makes this explicit by:

1. Indexing validity claims by S_{\mathrm{ref}},
2. Measuring displacement D_{\mathfrak V} when the reference changes,
3. Treating the reference as a design choice rather than a given.

This is analogous to the role of the cutoff in effective field theory: the cutoff is not a fundamental constant but a parameter that defines the regime of validity. Different cutoffs yield different effective theories, and the choice depends on the scale of interest. Similarly, different reference models yield different validity profiles, and the choice depends on the intervention class and outcome.

The regress is halted in practice when the intervention semantics become unambiguous at some level. For neural networks, this is the level of activations. For the linear benchmark, this is the level of state variables and external inputs, where interventions are defined by direct manipulation. The regress remains as a philosophical boundary condition, but it does not impede empirical progress.

