Appendix P: Connection to Mechanistic Interpretability

Mechanistic interpretability (MI) aims to reverse-engineer neural networks into human-understandable components: circuits, features, attention heads, and residual stream directions. MISA provides a formal framework for evaluating MI claims.

Let S_{\mathrm{ref}} be the neural network, O the behavior of interest (e.g., next-token prediction, refusal, factual recall), and I the intervention class (activation patching, ablation, steering). An MI explanation \mathcal A is a proposed abstraction. Its validity domain I_\epsilon(\mathcal A) is the set of interventions for which the explanation's causal predictions match the network's behavior within tolerance.

MISA predicts that different MI abstractions will have different validity domains:

· Circuit-level abstractions may be valid for activation patching at specific locations but fail under distributed steering.
· Feature-level abstractions (sparse autoencoders) may be valid for feature steering but fail under individual neuron ablation.
· Attention-head abstractions may be valid for head ablation but fail under residual stream interventions.

The containment test I_{\mathrm{req}}(O) \subseteq I_\epsilon(\mathcal A) determines whether a proposed explanation is adequate for the behavioral claim being made. This gives MI a falsifiable evaluation criterion that goes beyond anecdotal circuit discovery.

Recent work has begun to formalize these connections using categorical causal abstraction, and benchmarks such as MIB (Mechanistic Interpretability Benchmark) provide standardized intervention protocols that can be used to estimate I_\epsilon(\mathcal A) empirically.

