Q.1 Cross-Abstraction Prediction

H1. For \kappa sufficiently large, \mathcal A_1 will fail the containment test for O_2 (long-horizon state prediction) even when it passes for O_1 (one-step prediction). This tests the horizon-dependence of validity.

Q.2 Reduced-Mode Prediction

H2. \mathcal A_3 will be on the Pareto frontier for intermediate \kappa and T values, but off the frontier for extreme values. This tests the existence of an intermediate reduced-representation regime.

Q.3 Composition Prediction

H3. The empirical composition boundary will be approximately \|J_{\mathrm{cross}}\|_O = \epsilon, with a width proportional to the estimation uncertainty in \Delta_{\mathcal A}.

Q.4 Reference-Model Prediction

H4. The validity-profile displacement D_{\mathfrak V} will be larger for \mathcal A_1 than for \mathcal A_2, because \mathcal A_1 is more sensitive to unmodeled cross-coupling.

Q.5 Statistical Prediction

H5. Without multiple-comparison correction, the naive containment test will falsely declare validity with probability approximately 1 - (1-\alpha)^m, where m = |I_{\mathrm{req}}|. With correction, the false declaration rate will be controlled at \beta.

